// HostingWpRequestWithBody Edit a WordPress site: one REST API call, run inside the site
//
// **Applies to:** Cloud for WordPress (not the free preview).
//
// The way to change a WordPress site's content without credentials: one WordPress REST API call, e.g. `GET /wp/v2/pages` (list), `GET /wp/v2/pages/12` with `{"context": "edit"}` (raw block markup to edit), `POST /wp/v2/pages/12` with `{"content": "..."}` (update), `POST /wp/v2/posts` (create). Runs inside the site: WordPress's own REST handling is called in-process over WP-CLI, as a WordPress user, so no application password is created, stored or returned, and it works whatever the domain's DNS points at. By default it acts as the dedicated `porkbun-agent` editor (created on first use): pages, posts, media, comments, menus. Site settings (title, tagline, homepage), plugins, themes and users need an administrator: send `asAdministrator` and `acknowledgeFullAccess`. Each call takes a few seconds.
//
// The response is WordPress's own: `httpStatus`, `data` (its JSON), and `total`/`totalPages` on lists. A WordPress error (4xx/5xx) returns `status: ERROR`, `code: WP_REST_ERROR`, with WordPress's code and message in `data`. Answers over about 400 KB return `WP_RESPONSE_TOO_LARGE`: narrow them with `_fields`, `per_page` or a single id. Writes support `dryRun`. Rate limit: 600 calls per account per hour. Site-share keys (`site`) can use it.
//
// Agents that make their own HTTP requests can instead mint credentials with `/hosting/createWpCredentials` and call `https://{domain}/wp-json/` directly.
//
// **Errors:** `WP_REST_ERROR`, `WP_RESPONSE_TOO_LARGE`, `FULL_ACCESS_ACKNOWLEDGMENT_REQUIRED`, `WP_CLI_FAILED` (the site did not answer), `INVALID_INPUT`, `HOSTING_NOT_FOUND`, `PREVIEW_SITE_NOT_SUPPORTED`, `NOT_SUPPORTED_FOR_PRODUCT`.
