// TopupAccountCreditWithBodyWithResponse Top up account credit now
//
// **Charges the saved payment method** and adds the money to account credit immediately. The call that unblocks work already in progress: enabling auto top-up does nothing until the next order trips the threshold, which is no help to a caller holding an `INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS` response right now.
//
// **`amount` is optional.** Omitted, it charges the account's configured top-up amount, or $50 if the account has never set one — `amountSource` in the response says which of `configured`, `default` or `request` applied. Supplied, it charges that figure for this call only and leaves the stored setting alone, which is the point: an agent could always have written the amount to `/account/autoTopup` first, and making it do that turns a one-off charge into a silent edit of a setting the customer owns.
//
// The card itself can only be saved outside the API, and a supplied amount is held to the same 500–50000 cent range as one set through `/account/autoTopup`. On top of that the dollars are bounded by the month: the account's **monthly spend limit** caps top-ups as well as domain spend (it is the account saying how much the API may move, and a card charge is the API moving money), and an account that has never set one still gets a $100/month ceiling. Card, auto top-ups set off by an API purchase, MPP and USDC all count toward it — `monthlyCeiling` and `ceilingSource` on `GET /account/autoTopup` say which is in force. Top-ups are also limited to 5 per day and 20 per month (`TOPUP_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`), and every successful charge emails the account holder.
//
// **Primary and backup cards.** An account can save up to three cards on the website, one primary and the rest backups. A top-up charges the primary; if it is declined, it tries each backup in turn and charges at most one card. It does not fall back after a decline that suggests fraud (Radar blocks, lost or stolen cards). The response names the card that paid (`card`), sets `usedBackup` when a backup stepped in, and lists any card declined first in `declinedFirst`, so you can tell the account holder their primary card needs attention. `GET /account/autoTopup` lists the saved cards.
//
// Errors that matter: `NO_PAYMENT_METHOD` (nothing saved to charge, or every card is marked as no longer chargeable — the account holder has to add a card or buy credit on the website), `CARD_DECLINED` (every card tried was declined and nothing was added; `declined` lists each card with its decline code), `TOPUP_FAILED` (nothing was charged; retry once).
//
// With a sandbox key this grants simulated credit and charges nothing (`simulated: true`). Supports `dryRun`, which previews the amount and charges nothing in any environment. Honours `Idempotency-Key`.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /account/topup (the `TopupAccountCredit` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) TopupAccountCreditWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, params *TopupAccountCreditParams, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*TopupAccountCreditResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.TopupAccountCreditWithBody(ctx, params, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseTopupAccountCreditResponse(rsp)
}

